  "S4": {
   "id": "S4",
   "kind": "document",
   "title": "Ml Lecture Notes",
   "text": "4. Evaluation Metrics for Classification A confusion matrix counts true positives (TP), false positives (FP), true negatives (TN) and false negatives (FN). Accuracy is (TP + TN) divided by all examples. Accuracy is misleading on imbalanced data: a model that always predicts the majority class can reach 99% accuracy while detecting nothing. Precision = TP / (TP + FP) answers \"of the examples predicted positive, how many are truly positive?\". Recall (sensitivity) = TP / (TP + FN) answers \"of the truly positive examples, how many did we find?\". The F1 score is the harmonic mean of precision and recall: 2 * precision * recall / (precision + recall). Raising the decision threshold usually increases precision and lowers recall. The ROC curve plots the true positive rate against the false positive rate across thresholds, and AUC summarizes it: 0.5 is random guessing and 1.0 is perfect ranking.",
